# Frame-stage story rules.
Return exactly one <FRAME>...</FRAME> block per requested_frame_slots item, in the listed order. Each block contains one nonempty, unbroken prose paragraph. Output no JSON, IDs, Markdown fences, nested tags, explanations, or text outside the blocks. The program assigns all Frame IDs.
accepted_frames contains already saved alternatives for this Theme. Use their IDs to understand which requested slots remain; do not regenerate or return them. Complete only requested_frame_slots, even when retrying a subset.
Each Narrative Frame is one standalone renderable image and contains the complete visual deliverable required for that image. Any views, regions, panels, tiles, or repeated depictions specified by its layout are subdivisions of that one renderable image, not additional output Frames. Complete every required subdivision inside each Frame; do not spread one required image across Narrative Frames.
Repeated depictions of one named person inside a single image, including reflections or reference views, represent that same person and do not increase the cast. Preserve identity, appearance, clothing, and explicit continuity rules across those depictions.
Preserve the requested medium, rendering language, color behavior, scale relationships, projection, layout, typography, and image hierarchy exactly. Do not default to cinematic photography, a poster, a collage, a grid, or any other familiar format. Describe viewpoint and illumination in terms appropriate to that medium.
When the Story Description requires no fixed internal structure, write one unbroken natural-language paragraph without internal field labels. Establish style, era, country, place, and current moment naturally near the beginning.
Fully redescribe each visible principal person's adult identity, nationality, age, gender, stable appearance, hairstyle, clothing, expression, gaze, and current pose in every Frame. Describe explicitly permitted background adults at the detail their visibility supports, preserving their population bounds and adult status. Integrate these facts into visual reading order rather than listing a dossier.
Preserve the Theme's chosen identities and defining facts rather than inventing a second physical person with the same identity to meet a count. Explicitly requested reflections or repeated views remain depictions of one identity. Preserve established appearance and garment identity where consistent with mandatory system requirements and explicit Story Description constraints. Continuity rules do not replace those requirements.
Describe one still image or the explicitly requested set of views inside it, not sequential story steps or incompatible states within one depiction. A photographic exposure may visibly record motion through blur or light trails; those traces are not additional bodies or chronological Frames.
Within each depicted view, each person has one coherent body configuration appropriate to the physical setting. Assign every visible limb a consistent contact or force role. Grounded scenes require credible support; floating or zero-gravity scenes require coherent free-flight orientation, contact, and motion cues rather than invented ground contact. Body balance, limb direction, occlusion, clothing, and prop forces must agree with the declared environment.
Before writing, silently reconcile each visible hand's occupancy, arm reach, body support and object contact in that one instant. A hand or arm may have compatible simultaneous roles, but cannot support the body at one location while also reaching elsewhere to hold another object. Do not invent extra limbs or unsupported weight transfer to satisfy a pose.
Keep each garment's construction and visible state coherent: a one-piece garment does not become independently detachable upper and lower pieces unless that construction or alteration is explicitly established. Openings, sleeves, fastenings, fabric position and limb movement must agree within the same depiction.
Choose expression and gaze for each person's current action, effort and attention, rather than assigning everyone the same facial template. Show visible facial evidence, contacts and support; do not replace them with emotion labels or claims that the pose is physically correct.
Do not describe successive repositioning as a narrative inside one still depiction. Exposure traces and explicitly requested alternative views may show different visible positions without implying extra identities, and no body part may become an anatomically impossible structural element.
The visible state explicitly required by the Story Description must appear directly in every Frame. Do not replace it with a different preparation or aftermath, merely name it, or leave its defining evidence off frame.
Resolve conditional instructions only from the current request and Theme payload. Apply the branch whose condition matches current values and never borrow a branch assigned to another alternative.
Use setting objects, materials, distance, and spatial evidence that matter to the requested image rather than unrelated decoration.
Make viewpoint, framing, focus hierarchy, illumination direction, shadow behavior, and color treatment explicit in vocabulary appropriate to the requested medium.
Multiple Frames for one Theme are parallel visual alternatives that share stable Theme facts, not a chronological sequence. All alternatives depict an equivalent point in the Theme's action and one fixed time window; variation must not imply elapsed time or a before-and-after state change. Re-establish the complete image from scratch and vary only along axes allowed or required by the Story Description. Never refer to another Frame or use backward-pointing continuity shorthand.
Anchor every alternative to the concrete event or static visual proposition in premise and diversity.situation. Changing viewpoint or staging must not substitute a new problem, task, place or subject relationship. Do not turn a vague Theme into unrelated incidents across its Frames. Diversity is reference metadata, not text to recite or display in the image.
Follow the requested writing targets when supplied; otherwise include enough concrete renderable detail for the cast and visual complexity without a fixed word quota or padding.
Preserve visible image text specified by the Story Description verbatim inside double quotation marks. Do not invent visible text when none is specified.
Before submission, verify requested language, cast, physical coherence, presentation structure, and visible evidence. Rewrite any failure before returning.
Perform that check silently; return only complete image prose, not planning notes, compliance claims or statements about passing the checks.
